</figure>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx4.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Influence of Loss Terms</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx4.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx4.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we conduct ablation experiments on the target of <math id="Sx4.SSx4.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{noise}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{noise}</annotation></semantics></math>. The results are shown in Table <a href="#Sx4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Influence of Loss Terms ‣ Ablation Study ‣ Experiments ‣ End4: End-to-end Denoising Diffusion for Diffusion-Based Inpainting Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. We first ablate <math id="Sx4.SSx4.SSSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{noise}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{noise}</annotation></semantics></math>, which means the reconstruction model lacks guidance from the loss function. The results show that the model’s performance drops under this setting. Next, we compute <math id="Sx4.SSx4.SSSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{noise}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{noise}</annotation></semantics></math> for all images. The model’s performance decreases more significantly, suggesting that indiscriminate loss guidance for both nature and inpainting images is ineffective. Then, we apply <math id="Sx4.SSx4.SSSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{noise}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{noise}</annotation></semantics></math> to nature images and inpainting images, respectively. The results show that providing <math id="Sx4.SSx4.SSSx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{noise}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{noise}</annotation></semantics></math> only for inpainting images yields better performance. This aligns with the principle of diffusion generation detection based on reconstruction: generated images and their reconstructed images are more similar than real images and their reconstructions. These analyses indicate that selecting an appropriate target for <math id="Sx4.SSx4.SSSx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{noise}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{noise}</annotation></semantics></math> is crucial for the performance of the model.</p>
</div>
